WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.428
A repeatable intelligence analysis routine begins with the decision.

2
00:00:04.428 --> 00:00:12.103
Before collecting more information, define the intelligence question, audience, timeframe, and level of detail needed.

3
00:00:12.103 --> 00:00:17.343
Ask what decision the analysis should support and what would make the answer useful.

4
00:00:17.343 --> 00:00:23.247
This keeps the work focused and prevents collection from becoming a substitute for reasoning.

5
00:00:23.247 --> 00:00:26.273
Gather only authorized, relevant information.

6
00:00:26.273 --> 00:00:33.432
Use lawful, ethical, policy-approved sources such as public reports, internal authorized telemetry summaries,

7
00:00:33.432 --> 00:00:39.483
incident notes, vendor advisories, risk registers, business context, and sanitized case data.

8
00:00:39.483 --> 00:00:43.395
Protect sensitive information and minimize what you collect.

9
00:00:43.395 --> 00:00:50.185
Analysis quality does not require invading privacy or gathering information outside approved boundaries.

10
00:00:50.185 --> 00:00:52.399
Evaluate sources and information.

11
00:00:52.399 --> 00:00:56.236
Separate source reliability from information credibility.

12
00:00:56.236 --> 00:01:02.288
Ask whether the source has relevant access, whether the claim is plausible, whether it is timely,

13
00:01:02.288 --> 00:01:05.830
whether it is corroborated, and what limitations apply.

14
00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:10.554
Document those limits so others can review and update the assessment later.

15
00:01:10.554 --> 00:01:12.325
Organize evidence.

16
00:01:12.325 --> 00:01:19.557
Build a timeline, source table, evidence matrix, issue tracker, or structured note set that preserves provenance.

17
00:01:19.557 --> 00:01:24.281
Good organization reveals sequence, gaps, patterns, and contradictions.

18
00:01:24.281 --> 00:01:30.406
It also helps the team work under pressure without losing track of what is known and what is assumed.

19
00:01:30.406 --> 00:01:35.277
Separate facts, assumptions, hypotheses, judgments, and recommendations.

20
00:01:35.277 --> 00:01:37.048
Label assumptions.

21
00:01:37.048 --> 00:01:39.262
Test hypotheses against evidence.

22
00:01:39.262 --> 00:01:44.945
Use structured analytic techniques when the problem is complex, uncertain, or important.

23
00:01:44.945 --> 00:01:49.594
Consider alternatives and ask what evidence would change the assessment.

24
00:01:49.594 --> 00:01:55.425
This reduces the chance that the first plausible answer becomes the final answer too quickly.

25
00:01:55.425 --> 00:01:57.196
Communicate clearly.

26
00:01:57.196 --> 00:02:00.295
Lead with key judgments or bottom-line findings.

27
00:02:00.295 --> 00:02:05.978
Explain evidence, assumptions, confidence, uncertainty, implications, and next steps.

28
00:02:05.978 --> 00:02:08.118
Match the product to the audience.

29
00:02:08.118 --> 00:02:12.325
Avoid unsupported claims, vague wording, and hidden uncertainty.

30
00:02:12.325 --> 00:02:19.779
A good intelligence product makes reasoning visible enough that decision makers can act and reviewers can understand.

31
00:02:19.779 --> 00:02:21.550
Finally, review outcomes.

32
00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:28.930
After the decision, ask whether the analysis helped, what changed, what assumptions held, what evidence was missing,

33
00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:31.292
and what should be improved next time.

34
00:02:31.292 --> 00:02:36.384
Intelligence analysis is disciplined reasoning in service of better decisions.

35
00:02:36.384 --> 00:02:43.100
Its value comes from evidence, transparency, humility, and a constant effort to make judgment more useful.
